## Reached at
  A Flask + Socket.IO application (the `trape` project) staged by the image build
  into /app and launched from there as:
      python trape.py --url http://example.com --port 8080
  The HTTP service and the Socket.IO service share the same listener on port 8080
  (base URL http://localhost:8080, overridable via APP_URL). The behavioral target
  that every caller below reaches is the database module imported by name:
      from core.db import Database
      db = Database()
  instantiated as a module-level singleton inside core/stats.py, core/victim.py,
  and core/sockets.py. The application'"'"'s own database file lives at:
      /app/database.db
  (opened relative to the /app working directory inside Database.__init__ as
  sqlite3.connect("database.db", check_same_thread=False), and created/seeded by
  Database.loadDatabase on startup from trape.py). Callers reach the Database
  object exclusively through the app'"'"'s HTTP routes and one Socket.IO handler.

## Must provide
  Database                  class, exported from core/db.py, importable as
                            `from core.db import Database`; instantiated with no
                            arguments as `Database()`
  Database.loadDatabase     method, called once at startup with no arguments;
                            creates the geo, networks, requests, victims, clicks
                            tables in /app/database.db
  Database.sentences_stats  method (type, vId=None); reached by the stats routes
  Database.sentences_victim method (type, data=None, sRun=1, column=0); reached
                            by the victim routes and the socket disconnect handler
  HTTP routes (all project-owned, registered on the shared Flask app):
      POST /register         in core/victim.py
      POST /nr               in core/victim.py
      POST /regv             in core/victim.py
      POST /tping            in core/victim.py
      POST /get_data         in core/stats.py
      POST /get_preview      in core/stats.py
      POST /get_requests     in core/stats.py
  Socket.IO event handler (project-owned, namespace /trape):
      disconnect_request     in core/sockets.py

## Called as
  HTTP routes call the Database singleton as follows (each request'"'"'s form fields
  are the inputs whose type/shape matters; none are validated by the caller):

  POST /register  (core/victim.py register)
      request.form fields, all strings:
        vId, cpu, country, countryCode, query, lat, lon, org,
        region, regionName, timezone, zip, isp
      db.sentences_victim('"'"'count_times'"'"', vId, 3, 0)         # returns one row, int()-coerced
      db.sentences_victim('"'"'insert_click'"'"', [vId, url, ts], 2)
      db.sentences_victim('"'"'delete_networks'"'"', [vId], 2)
      # when the victim is new:
      db.sentences_victim('"'"'insert_victim'"'"',      [victimConnect, vId, ts_float], 2)
      db.sentences_victim('"'"'insert_victim_geo'"'"',  [victimGeo, vId], 2)
      # when the victim already exists:
      db.sentences_victim('"'"'update_victim'"'"',      [victimConnect, vId, ts_float], 2)
      db.sentences_victim('"'"'update_victim_geo'"'"',  [victimGeo, vId], 2)
      where victimConnect is a victim object and victimGeo a victim_geo object
      built from the form fields above.

  POST /nr  (core/victim.py networkRegister)
      request.form fields, all strings: vId, red, ip, public_ip
